    source: "03-projects/t3-04-a-process-gap-found-and-specced.md",
    summary: "Read first: your own T2-2 and",
    order: 460,
    words: 835,
    body: "# T3-4 · A process gap, found and specced\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Time box** | One to two days |\n| **Account** | Across the account groups you've worked — the negatives loop |\n| **Safety** | Read-only — you're mapping a process and specifying a fix, not building it |\n| **Paired or solo** | Solo, with a readout — the spec goes to Alex |\n| **Deliverable** | A coverage map of the loop plus a one-page spec for closing one gap → `04-my-work/projects/` |\n| **Builds toward** | [D · Operating rhythm](../05-self-assessment/baseline.md#d--operating-rhythm) · [E · Judgment](../05-self-assessment/baseline.md#e--judgment) |\n\n**Read first:** your own [T2-2](./t2-02-the-same-drill-at-scale.md) and\n[M-3](./m-03-attach-and-build-the-negatives.md) write-ups.\n\n---\n\n## The point\n\nT3-2 asked where money leaks. This asks a different question about the same machinery: **where\nis the process that's supposed to catch that money thin, silent, or simply not running?**\n\nThe negatives loop is the recurring process you know best — you've fed it (T2-2) and you've\nseen an account where a negative list existed but was **attached to nothing** and so did\nnothing at all (M-3, on Mecco). That last one is the shape you're hunting at scale: a control\nthat looks present and isn't actually working.\n\nA process gap is worth more than a single waste finding because it **keeps producing waste\nuntil someone closes it.** Finding one, mapping it, and writing down what it'd take to fix is\nexactly the kind of second-year, high-leverage work `after-the-ramp.md` points at — you're\njust doing the *diagnose-and-spec* half of it now, not the build.\n\n---\n\n## What to do\n\n### 1 · Map where the loop actually runs\n\nFor each account group you can see, establish the truth, not the assumption:\n\n| Question | How you'd tell |\n|---|---|\n| **Does the loop run here at all?** | Is there a live search-terms review process, or does nothing sweep this account group? |\n| **If it runs, does it *reach* the account?** | The M-3 trap — a list can exist and be attached to nothing. Check the negatives are actually attached to serving campaigns, not just sitting in a shared library |\n| **Does it cover the whole group?** | New campaigns, new ad groups — do they inherit the protection, or do they start naked until someone remembers? |\n\nWrite it as a simple coverage grid: account group × runs / reaches / covers-new. The blanks\nare your candidates.\n\n### 2 · Pick the one that costs the most, and confirm it's real\n\nDon't spec the first blank you find — spec the one leaking the most. A group with no loop and\nreal spend beats a cosmetic gap on a dormant account. Confirm the gap is genuine before you\nbuild a case on it: \"I think this isn't covered\" becomes \"here are three recent expensive\nterms that would have been caught and weren't.\"\n\n### 3 · Spec the fix — for a human reader, not a machine\n\nOne page: **what the gap is, what it's costing (with evidence), and what it would take to\nclose it.** Not code. The spec is \"here's the hole, here's the size of it, here's roughly the\nwork\" — enough for Alex to decide whether it's worth doing and who does it.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Absence is hard to prove — say how sure you are\n\nThe whole project is about finding things that *aren't* there, and \"I couldn't find it\" and\n\"it doesn't exist\" are different claims. You may be looking in the wrong place, or a process\nmay run somewhere you can't see. So grade your confidence: *\"no coverage that I can find, and\nI checked A, B and C\"* is honest and useful. *\"No coverage\"* stated flat may just be a gap in\nyour access. When you're not sure, the finding is a **question for Alex**, not a conclusion.\n\n## ⚠️ You're specifying, not building — and definitely not attaching anything live\n\nAttaching a negative list, changing a shared library, wiring up a review — those are live\nchanges with the silent-over-blocking risk you already know. This project stops at the spec.\nThe building is a real piece of work with its own review; don't fold it into this one because\nyou can see how to do it.\n\n---\n\n## You're done when\n\n- [ ] A coverage grid distinguishing **doesn't run / runs but doesn't reach / runs but doesn't cover new campaigns** — three gaps, three fixes\n- [ ] The gap you specced is the one with the **most spend behind it**, proven with specific recent terms — not a hunch\n- [ ] A one-page spec: the hole, its size (with evidence), and roughly the work — a manager could decide go/no-go without three follow-ups\n- [ ] Confidence graded on any absence (\"no coverage I can find, checked A/B/C\") — unsure findings raised as **questions**, not conclusions\n- [ ] You **specced, didn't build** — nothing attached or wired live\n- [ ] Standard wrap"
  },
